{"format":"kustomlearn","version":1,"course":{"title":"SOC Analyst from Zero","request":"SOC analyst from zero, about an hour a day","intent":"Get hands-on with SOC analyst from zero, about an hour a d","category":"Cybersecurity / SOC","variation":{"labs":{"code":1,"logs":4,"terminal":3},"name":"Fast track","level":"beginner","pitch":"Triage real alerts within two weeks.","weeks":2,"modules":6,"capstone":false,"outcomes":["Read Windows event logs","Spot a brute-force attack","Write a triage note"],"hoursPerWeek":5},"plan":{"title":"SOC Analyst from Zero","modules":[{"title":"Module 1: Logs 101","summary":"What it is, why analysts care, and how to spot it.","objectives":["Explain the core idea","Find it in real logs"]},{"title":"Module 2: Windows events","summary":"What it is, why analysts care, and how to spot it.","objectives":["Explain the core idea","Find it in real logs"]},{"title":"Module 3: Brute force","summary":"What it is, why analysts care, and how to spot it.","objectives":["Explain the core idea","Find it in real logs"]},{"title":"Module 4: Lateral movement","summary":"What it is, why analysts care, and how to spot it.","objectives":["Explain the core idea","Find it in real logs"]},{"title":"Module 5: Detections","summary":"What it is, why analysts care, and how to spot it.","objectives":["Explain the core idea","Find it in real logs"]},{"title":"Module 6: Triage notes","summary":"What it is, why analysts care, and how to spot it.","objectives":["Explain the core idea","Find it in real logs"]}]}},"modules":[{"title":"Module 1: Logs 101","content":{"labs":[],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"choices":["4624","4625","4688","4720"],"question":"Which Windows event ID is a failed logon?","explanation":"4625 records a failed logon attempt."}],"diagram":"flowchart LR\n  A[Alert] --> B[Triage] --> C{Real?}\n  C -->|yes| D[Escalate]\n  C -->|no| E[Close]","sections":[{"heading":"Why it matters","markdown":"**Module 1: Logs 101** shows up in almost every incident."},{"heading":"Try it","markdown":"```bash\ngrep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log | wc -l\n```"}]}},{"title":"Module 2: Windows events","content":{"labs":[{"kind":"logs","task":"The `auth_log` table holds SSH logins. Which source IPs failed to log in more than 5 times? Return `ip` and the number of failures.","hints":["Only the failed logins matter here.","Group the failed rows by `ip` and count them.","Filter groups with `having count(*) > 5`."],"setup":"create table auth_log (ts timestamptz not null, host text not null, username text not null, ip text not null, ok boolean not null);\ninsert into auth_log\nselect timestamptz '2026-11-01 08:00Z' + (n * interval '7 minutes'), 'web-0' || (n % 3), (array['amy','bob','cat','dev'])[1 + n % 4], '10.0.0.' || (1 + n % 9), n % 5 <> 0\nfrom generate_series(1, 120) n;\ninsert into auth_log\nselect timestamptz '2026-11-02 03:00Z' + (n * interval '20 seconds'), 'web-01', 'root', '203.0.113.7', false\nfrom generate_series(1, 14) n;","title":"Find the brute-force source","ordered":false,"starter":"select ip, ok\nfrom auth_log\nlimit 10","expected":{"rows":[["203.0.113.7","14"]],"columns":["ip","failures"]},"solution":"select ip, count(*) as failures from auth_log where not ok group by ip having count(*) > 5","walkthrough":"Keep only failed logins (`where not ok`), group them by `ip`, and keep the groups with more than 5 rows (`having count(*) > 5`). The attacker's IP stands out with 14 failures in under 5 minutes."},{"kind":"terminal","task":"`/var/log/auth.log` holds a night of SSH logins. Save every source IP with a failed password, once each and sorted, to `~/case/ips.txt`.","files":[{"path":"/var/log/auth.log","content":"Nov  2 03:00:01 web-01 sshd[811]: Failed password for root from 203.0.113.7 port 52110 ssh2\nNov  2 03:00:04 web-01 sshd[812]: Accepted password for amy from 10.0.0.2 port 40112 ssh2\nNov  2 03:00:09 web-01 sshd[813]: Failed password for admin from 203.0.113.7 port 52114 ssh2\nNov  2 03:01:12 web-01 sshd[820]: Failed password for bob from 198.51.100.23 port 61002 ssh2\nNov  2 03:02:40 web-01 sshd[826]: Accepted password for cat from 10.0.0.5 port 40190 ssh2\n"},{"path":"/home/learner/README.txt","content":"Put your findings in ~/case.\n"}],"hints":["Start by making the `case` directory with `mkdir`.","`grep 'Failed password'` keeps only the failures; `cut` can pick one field.","Finish the pipe with `sort -u` and redirect it with `> case/ips.txt`."],"title":"Pull the attacker's IP","checks":[{"kind":"exists","name":"case folder exists","path":"~/case","text":"dir"},{"kind":"contains","name":"ips.txt has the attacker","path":"~/case/ips.txt","text":"203.0.113.7"},{"kind":"contains","name":"ips.txt has the second IP","path":"~/case/ips.txt","text":"198.51.100.23"},{"kind":"lacks","name":"no successful logins listed","path":"~/case/ips.txt","text":"10.0.0"}],"solution":["mkdir case","grep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log | cut -d' ' -f12 | sort -u > case/ips.txt","cat case/ips.txt"],"walkthrough":"`grep` keeps the failed lines, `cut -d' ' -f12` takes the IP field (the double space after `Nov` counts as an empty field), `sort -u` sorts and drops repeats, and `>` saves the result."}],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"choices":["4624","4625","4688","4720"],"question":"Which Windows event ID is a failed logon?","explanation":"4625 records a failed logon attempt."}],"diagram":"flowchart LR\n  A[Alert] --> B[Triage] --> C{Real?}\n  C -->|yes| D[Escalate]\n  C -->|no| E[Close]","sections":[{"heading":"Why it matters","markdown":"**Module 2: Windows events** shows up in almost every incident."},{"heading":"Try it","markdown":"```bash\ngrep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log | wc -l\n```"}]}},{"title":"Module 3: Brute force","content":{"labs":[{"kind":"logs","task":"The `auth_log` table holds SSH logins. Which source IPs failed to log in more than 5 times? Return `ip` and the number of failures.","hints":["Only the failed logins matter here.","Group the failed rows by `ip` and count them.","Filter groups with `having count(*) > 5`."],"setup":"create table auth_log (ts timestamptz not null, host text not null, username text not null, ip text not null, ok boolean not null);\ninsert into auth_log\nselect timestamptz '2026-11-01 08:00Z' + (n * interval '7 minutes'), 'web-0' || (n % 3), (array['amy','bob','cat','dev'])[1 + n % 4], '10.0.0.' || (1 + n % 9), n % 5 <> 0\nfrom generate_series(1, 120) n;\ninsert into auth_log\nselect timestamptz '2026-11-02 03:00Z' + (n * interval '20 seconds'), 'web-01', 'root', '203.0.113.7', false\nfrom generate_series(1, 14) n;","title":"Find the brute-force source","ordered":false,"starter":"select ip, ok\nfrom auth_log\nlimit 10","expected":{"rows":[["203.0.113.7","14"]],"columns":["ip","failures"]},"solution":"select ip, count(*) as failures from auth_log where not ok group by ip having count(*) > 5","walkthrough":"Keep only failed logins (`where not ok`), group them by `ip`, and keep the groups with more than 5 rows (`having count(*) > 5`). The attacker's IP stands out with 14 failures in under 5 minutes."}],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"choices":["4624","4625","4688","4720"],"question":"Which Windows event ID is a failed logon?","explanation":"4625 records a failed logon attempt."}],"diagram":"flowchart LR\n  A[Alert] --> B[Triage] --> C{Real?}\n  C -->|yes| D[Escalate]\n  C -->|no| E[Close]","sections":[{"heading":"Why it matters","markdown":"**Module 3: Brute force** shows up in almost every incident."},{"heading":"Try it","markdown":"```bash\ngrep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log | wc -l\n```"}]}},{"title":"Module 4: Lateral movement","content":{"labs":[{"kind":"terminal","task":"`/var/log/auth.log` holds a night of SSH logins. Save every source IP with a failed password, once each and sorted, to `~/case/ips.txt`.","files":[{"path":"/var/log/auth.log","content":"Nov  2 03:00:01 web-01 sshd[811]: Failed password for root from 203.0.113.7 port 52110 ssh2\nNov  2 03:00:04 web-01 sshd[812]: Accepted password for amy from 10.0.0.2 port 40112 ssh2\nNov  2 03:00:09 web-01 sshd[813]: Failed password for admin from 203.0.113.7 port 52114 ssh2\nNov  2 03:01:12 web-01 sshd[820]: Failed password for bob from 198.51.100.23 port 61002 ssh2\nNov  2 03:02:40 web-01 sshd[826]: Accepted password for cat from 10.0.0.5 port 40190 ssh2\n"},{"path":"/home/learner/README.txt","content":"Put your findings in ~/case.\n"}],"hints":["Start by making the `case` directory with `mkdir`.","`grep 'Failed password'` keeps only the failures; `cut` can pick one field.","Finish the pipe with `sort -u` and redirect it with `> case/ips.txt`."],"title":"Pull the attacker's IP","checks":[{"kind":"exists","name":"case folder exists","path":"~/case","text":"dir"},{"kind":"contains","name":"ips.txt has the attacker","path":"~/case/ips.txt","text":"203.0.113.7"},{"kind":"contains","name":"ips.txt has the second IP","path":"~/case/ips.txt","text":"198.51.100.23"},{"kind":"lacks","name":"no successful logins listed","path":"~/case/ips.txt","text":"10.0.0"}],"solution":["mkdir case","grep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log | cut -d' ' -f12 | sort -u > case/ips.txt","cat case/ips.txt"],"walkthrough":"`grep` keeps the failed lines, `cut -d' ' -f12` takes the IP field (the double space after `Nov` counts as an empty field), `sort -u` sorts and drops repeats, and `>` saves the result."}],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"choices":["4624","4625","4688","4720"],"question":"Which Windows event ID is a failed logon?","explanation":"4625 records a failed logon attempt."}],"diagram":"flowchart LR\n  A[Alert] --> B[Triage] --> C{Real?}\n  C -->|yes| D[Escalate]\n  C -->|no| E[Close]","sections":[{"heading":"Why it matters","markdown":"**Module 4: Lateral movement** shows up in almost every incident."},{"heading":"Try it","markdown":"```bash\ngrep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log | wc -l\n```"}]}},{"title":"Module 5: Detections","content":{"labs":[{"kind":"logs","task":"The `auth_log` table holds SSH logins. Which source IPs failed to log in more than 5 times? Return `ip` and the number of failures.","hints":["Only the failed logins matter here.","Group the failed rows by `ip` and count them.","Filter groups with `having count(*) > 5`."],"setup":"create table auth_log (ts timestamptz not null, host text not null, username text not null, ip text not null, ok boolean not null);\ninsert into auth_log\nselect timestamptz '2026-11-01 08:00Z' + (n * interval '7 minutes'), 'web-0' || (n % 3), (array['amy','bob','cat','dev'])[1 + n % 4], '10.0.0.' || (1 + n % 9), n % 5 <> 0\nfrom generate_series(1, 120) n;\ninsert into auth_log\nselect timestamptz '2026-11-02 03:00Z' + (n * interval '20 seconds'), 'web-01', 'root', '203.0.113.7', false\nfrom generate_series(1, 14) n;","title":"Find the brute-force source","ordered":false,"starter":"select ip, ok\nfrom auth_log\nlimit 10","expected":{"rows":[["203.0.113.7","14"]],"columns":["ip","failures"]},"solution":"select ip, count(*) as failures from auth_log where not ok group by ip having count(*) > 5","walkthrough":"Keep only failed logins (`where not ok`), group them by `ip`, and keep the groups with more than 5 rows (`having count(*) > 5`). The attacker's IP stands out with 14 failures in under 5 minutes."}],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"choices":["4624","4625","4688","4720"],"question":"Which Windows event ID is a failed logon?","explanation":"4625 records a failed logon attempt."}],"diagram":"flowchart LR\n  A[Alert] --> B[Triage] --> C{Real?}\n  C -->|yes| D[Escalate]\n  C -->|no| E[Close]","sections":[{"heading":"Why it matters","markdown":"**Module 5: Detections** shows up in almost every incident."},{"heading":"Try it","markdown":"```bash\ngrep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log | wc -l\n```"}]}},{"title":"Module 6: Triage notes","content":{"labs":[{"kind":"logs","task":"The `auth_log` table holds SSH logins. Which source IPs failed to log in more than 5 times? Return `ip` and the number of failures.","hints":["Only the failed logins matter here.","Group the failed rows by `ip` and count them.","Filter groups with `having count(*) > 5`."],"setup":"create table auth_log (ts timestamptz not null, host text not null, username text not null, ip text not null, ok boolean not null);\ninsert into auth_log\nselect timestamptz '2026-11-01 08:00Z' + (n * interval '7 minutes'), 'web-0' || (n % 3), (array['amy','bob','cat','dev'])[1 + n % 4], '10.0.0.' || (1 + n % 9), n % 5 <> 0\nfrom generate_series(1, 120) n;\ninsert into auth_log\nselect timestamptz '2026-11-02 03:00Z' + (n * interval '20 seconds'), 'web-01', 'root', '203.0.113.7', false\nfrom generate_series(1, 14) n;","title":"Find the brute-force source","ordered":false,"starter":"select ip, ok\nfrom auth_log\nlimit 10","expected":{"rows":[["203.0.113.7","14"]],"columns":["ip","failures"]},"solution":"select ip, count(*) as failures from auth_log where not ok group by ip having count(*) > 5","walkthrough":"Keep only failed logins (`where not ok`), group them by `ip`, and keep the groups with more than 5 rows (`having count(*) > 5`). The attacker's IP stands out with 14 failures in under 5 minutes."},{"kind":"code","task":"Write `failures_by_user(events)`. Each event is `{\"user\": str, \"ok\": bool}`. Return a dict from each user to how many of their events failed, leaving out users with none.\n\nExample: `[{\"user\": \"amy\", \"ok\": false}, {\"user\": \"bob\", \"ok\": true}]` gives `{\"amy\": 1}`.","entry":"failures_by_user","hints":["Loop over the events and skip the successful ones.","Keep a dict and add one for the user of each failure.","`counts.get(user, 0) + 1` starts a new user at 1."],"tests":[{"args":"[[]]","name":"no events"},{"args":"[[{\"user\":\"amy\",\"ok\":false},{\"user\":\"bob\",\"ok\":true}]]","name":"one failure"},{"args":"[[{\"user\":\"amy\",\"ok\":false},{\"user\":\"amy\",\"ok\":false},{\"user\":\"cat\",\"ok\":false}]]","name":"repeat offender"},{"args":"[[{\"user\":\"amy\",\"ok\":true}]]","name":"all fine"}],"title":"Count failures per user","starter":"def failures_by_user(events):\n    counts = {}\n    # your code here\n    return counts\n","expected":["{}","{\"amy\":1}","{\"amy\":2,\"cat\":1}","{}"],"language":"python","solution":"def failures_by_user(events):\n    counts = {}\n    for e in events:\n        if not e['ok']:\n            counts[e['user']] = counts.get(e['user'], 0) + 1\n    return counts\n","walkthrough":"Walk the events once. For each failure, add one to that user's count, starting from 0 with `dict.get`. Users who never fail never get a key."},{"kind":"terminal","task":"`/var/log/auth.log` holds a night of SSH logins. Save every source IP with a failed password, once each and sorted, to `~/case/ips.txt`.","files":[{"path":"/var/log/auth.log","content":"Nov  2 03:00:01 web-01 sshd[811]: Failed password for root from 203.0.113.7 port 52110 ssh2\nNov  2 03:00:04 web-01 sshd[812]: Accepted password for amy from 10.0.0.2 port 40112 ssh2\nNov  2 03:00:09 web-01 sshd[813]: Failed password for admin from 203.0.113.7 port 52114 ssh2\nNov  2 03:01:12 web-01 sshd[820]: Failed password for bob from 198.51.100.23 port 61002 ssh2\nNov  2 03:02:40 web-01 sshd[826]: Accepted password for cat from 10.0.0.5 port 40190 ssh2\n"},{"path":"/home/learner/README.txt","content":"Put your findings in ~/case.\n"}],"hints":["Start by making the `case` directory with `mkdir`.","`grep 'Failed password'` keeps only the failures; `cut` can pick one field.","Finish the pipe with `sort -u` and redirect it with `> case/ips.txt`."],"title":"Pull the attacker's IP","checks":[{"kind":"exists","name":"case folder exists","path":"~/case","text":"dir"},{"kind":"contains","name":"ips.txt has the attacker","path":"~/case/ips.txt","text":"203.0.113.7"},{"kind":"contains","name":"ips.txt has the second IP","path":"~/case/ips.txt","text":"198.51.100.23"},{"kind":"lacks","name":"no successful logins listed","path":"~/case/ips.txt","text":"10.0.0"}],"solution":["mkdir case","grep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log | cut -d' ' -f12 | sort -u > case/ips.txt","cat case/ips.txt"],"walkthrough":"`grep` keeps the failed lines, `cut -d' ' -f12` takes the IP field (the double space after `Nov` counts as an empty field), `sort -u` sorts and drops repeats, and `>` saves the result."}],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"choices":["4624","4625","4688","4720"],"question":"Which Windows event ID is a failed logon?","explanation":"4625 records a failed logon attempt."}],"diagram":"flowchart LR\n  A[Alert] --> B[Triage] --> C{Real?}\n  C -->|yes| D[Escalate]\n  C -->|no| E[Close]","sections":[{"heading":"Why it matters","markdown":"**Module 6: Triage notes** shows up in almost every incident."},{"heading":"Try it","markdown":"```bash\ngrep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log | wc -l\n```"}]}}]}